def zip_with[A: Movable & Deinitable, B: Movable & Deinitable, R: Movable & Deinitable, V: Movable & Deinitable, W: Movable & Deinitable](var function: def(var A, var B) thin -> R, var source: V, var other: W) -> ZipWithIterator[A, B, R, _Source[V], _Source[W], def(var A, var B) thin -> R] where _Source[V].Element == A and _Source[W].Element == B:
    """Lazily combine pairs using native left-to-right zip followed by map.

    Construction is inert; each output moves its inputs into the non-raising callback.
    Native zip can consume an earlier input before a later one ends.
    Mapping fuses exhaustion and retains owned captures, move-only results and native origins.

    Parameters:
        A: The first callback argument type.
        B: The second callback argument type.
        R: The callback result type.
        V: The consumed native iterator or owned collection type.
        W: The second consumed source type.

    Args:
        function: The callback, borrowed or owned according to the selected overload; captures retain their native origins.
        source: The source, consumed at its current position.
        other: The second source, consumed at its current position.

    Returns:
        ZipWithIterator[A, B, R, _Source[V], _Source[W], def(var A, var B) thin -> R] with the ownership, timing and empty-input behavior described above.
    """
    var source_iter = _native_zip(source^, other^)
    return ZipWithIterator[A, B, R, _Source[V], _Source[W], def(var A, var B) thin -> R](function, source_iter^, False)

